From 4fab807c972c4e4ad856b4a9c59a561218e8e09d Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Aadi Srivastava <aadisrivastava.iitm@gmail.com>
Date: Tue, 6 Oct 2026 12:37:48 +0000
Subject: [PATCH 03/24] Carry induction variables whose stride is
 loop-invariant

The strength reduction added earlier carries `invariant + m * counter + c` for
an integer constant `m`.  A loop over the rows of a matrix is not that shape:
the base of row `r` is `r * columns`, which steps by the loop-invariant
`columns` rather than by a constant, so the pass declined it and every row
rebuilt the multiplication.  Worse, that index is what the interior pointers
are formed from, so the row loop also recomputed all four row pointers from
scratch: 25 instructions of preamble, including an `imulq` and eight `leaq`s,
in front of an inner loop of 13.

This generalises the analysis from an affine function with integer coefficients
to a linear form whose coefficients are expressions: a value is
`pointer + (stride * counter + offset)` where `stride` and `offset` are built
only from constants and from variables bound outside the whole `Ccatch`.  The
step is then one addition of a quantity bound once before the loop, and because
the row pointers of a loop all advance by the same number of bytes, that
quantity is shared between them.

The values actually carried are the row pointers, not the row indices.  A
candidate that nothing reads once the others are carried is dropped, which is
exactly the index a carried pointer was built from; carrying both instead costs
four registers and spills the hot loop.  `-no-cmm-carried-pointers` restricts
the pass to integers and shows that.

A carried pointer is live across the back edge, which is where a poll would go,
so `Cfg_selectgen` refuses an `Addr` in a `Ccatch` register.  That refusal is
relaxed under exactly the condition the interior pointers already require --
the unit is compiled without polling -- and the pass requires in addition that
the loop cannot allocate.  `emit.ml` still rejects an `Addr` live at any frame
descriptor, so a mistake in either remains a compile error rather than heap
corruption.  Hoisting the stride is sound for the same reason: it reads its
operands once instead of once per iteration, which is the same computation only
because nothing can move them in between.

On the microgpt inference benchmark (4-way unrolled kernel, `-O3 -unsafe`,
21 interleaved rounds, pinned): +4.4% over the interior pointers alone, faster
in 17 of 21 rounds, output bit-identical.  The row-group preamble drops from 25
instructions to 13 and the `imulq` leaves the loop.  Cumulatively with the two
earlier passes, +29.9% over pristine 5.4.0+ox in 21 of 21 rounds, which closes
the gap to the same C++ kernel compiled with the vectoriser off from 1.29x to
1.23x.

Off with -no-cmm-invariant-strides; pointers alone with
-no-cmm-carried-pointers.
